|~ `::path`, the kind of a path, and what is declared under it: its
   verb and the refusals of a read that misses.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence], [QSPTH#value]. ~|

:path
  |~~ A path, `/a/0/b`, is a value that names a part of another: a key
      of a map or an index of a vector at each segment, a negative index
      counted from the end. It does nothing by itself; the verb that
      takes it reads it. `get /a/b` answers the part and refuses one
      that is not there, so a misspelled field is found where it is
      read; `query /a/b` answers null there; `has /a/b` answers whether
      it is there; `put /a/b 2`, `update /a/b ~(add 1)` and `delete /a/b`
      write it, followed as `get` follows it; and a verb that runs code
      takes a path as the code
      that reads it from each element, `map /name`, `sort /age`,
      `filter /active`. A path prints in one spelling that reads back
      as itself, `/"0"` printing `/0`, and stands as any value does,
      `/items/1 | segments` answering `[:items 1]`.

      A key is written as a keyword is, bare, `/name`, quoted,
      `/"first name"`, or with its colon when it holds a slash,
      `/:qlang/error`, one key where `/qlang/error` is two. A segment is
      text, as in JSON Pointer, so `/0` reads the index of a vector and
      the key `"0"` of a map. The value itself is `it`, never a path.
      ::snippet~({:a {:b [5 6]}} | get /a/b/-1) answers `6`.
      ::link~(::any/get | doc) ::link~(::any/query | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps
        |~~ A path, read as itself; a vector of keys and indices becomes
            one by `toPath`, `[:items 0] | toPath`.
        ~~|
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::path` reads its payload as the path it is; a payload of
            another kind is refused, and a vector of keys and indices is
            read as a path by `toPath`.
            ::link~(::vec/toPath | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :IndexOutOfBoundsError
        |~~ An index reads within a vector or a set, from `/0` to the last,
            `/-1`, a negative index counting from the end; `:index` holds
            the index read and `:length` the length of the subject. The
            last element is `/-1` whatever the length, an index that may
            lie past the end is read by `query`, which answers null there,
            and `take` and `drop` answer the part there is without
            refusing.
            ::link~(::path | doc) ::link~(::any/query | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :IndexOutOfBoundsError/index
        |~~ The index the path read, counted from the end when negative. ~~|
        ::number
    | carries :IndexOutOfBoundsError/length
        |~~ How many elements the value holds. ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :KeyNotInMapError
        |~~ A path names a key the map holds, so a misspelled field is
            found where `get` reads it; `:key` holds the key read, and
            `:nearest` the keys of the map it was likely meant to be, near
            it in spelling as a name is near the names of the scope.
            `keys` lists the keys there are, `{:a 1} | keys`, and a key
            that may be absent is read by `query`, which answers null for
            it or the default it is given, `query /b 0`. A path read from
            the subject of a later step reads what that step was handed:
            after `get /dependencies |` the subject is the dependencies,
            so two fields of one record are read in one step,
            `get [/dependencies /devDependencies]`.
            ::link~(::path | doc) ::link~(::any/query | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :KeyNotInMapError/key
        |~~ The key the path read, which the map does not hold. ~~|
        ::keyword
    | throws :SubjectHasNoPartsError
        |~~ A path reads a part of a map, a vector or a set, one under a
            tag among them; a number, a string or null has no parts, and
            `:segment` holds the segment read, a key or an index. A path
            read from the subject of a later step reads what that step was
            handed: after `get /qty |` the subject is the number, so two
            fields of one record are read in one step, `get [/qty /price]`,
            or combined by a verb that reads each from its args,
            `mulOf /qty /price`. A value that may be null is read by
            `query`, which answers null.
            ::link~(::path | doc) ::link~(::any/mulOf | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :SubjectHasNoPartsError/segment
        |~~ The segment the path read, a key or an index. ~~|
        #[::keyword ::number]
    | throws :IndexNotIntegerError
        |~~ A vector is read by an index, `/0`, and a set by the index of
            an element in its one order; `:segment` holds the segment read,
            which names no index. A field of each element is read by `map`,
            `map /name`, and the elements a field picks are found by
            `filter`, `filter ~(get /n | gt 1)`.
            ::link~(::path | doc) ::link~(::vec/filter | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :IndexNotIntegerError/segment
        |~~ The segment the path read, which names no index. ~~|
        ::keyword
    | proves :isAValue
        |~~ A path is a value of its own kind. ~~|
        ~(/items/0 | type | eq ::path)
    | proves :printsInOneSpelling
        |~~ A path prints in one spelling that reads back as itself. ~~|
        ~([/items/0/"first name" /"0" /:name] | map ~print | eq ["/items/0/\"first name\"" "/0" "/name"])
    | proves :readsQuotedAndPathKeys
        |~~ Quoted keys and keys with a path read in a path. ~~|
        ~({:"foo bar" {:qlang/error 1}} | get /"foo bar"/:qlang/error | eq 1)
    | proves :readsNestedKeys
        |~~ A path of two keys reads nested maps. ~~|
        ~({:qlang {:error 2}} | get /qlang/error | eq 2)
    | proves :readsTheKeyOfAMapByItsDigits
        |~~ A segment of digits reads the key of a map that spells it. ~~|
        ~({:"0" 5} | get /0 | eq 5)
    | proves :printsAKeyWithASlashQuoted
        |~~ A key with a slash that is no name of a path prints quoted. ~~|
        ~({:"a b/c" 1} | paths | first | first | print | eq "/\"a b/c\"")
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector payload is refused; `toPath` reads one as a path. ~~|
        ~([:a 0] | tag ::path !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::path/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::vec])
    | proves :IndexOutOfBoundsError/refusesAnIndexPastTheEnd
        |~~ An index past the end is refused with the length. ~~|
        ~([10 20 30] | get /99 !| get [~(type) /index /length] | eq [::path/IndexOutOfBoundsError 99 3])
    | proves :IndexOutOfBoundsError/refusesAnIndexBeforeTheStart
        |~~ A negative index past the start is refused as it was written.
        ~~|
        ~([10 20 30] | get /-4 !| get [~(type) /index /length] | eq [::path/IndexOutOfBoundsError -4 3])
    | proves :IndexOutOfBoundsError/readsTheLastFromTheEnd
        |~~ `/-1` reads the last element whatever the length. ~~|
        ~([10 20 30] | get /-1 | eq 30)
    | proves :KeyNotInMapError/refusesAKeyTheMapLacks
        |~~ A key the map lacks is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | get /b !| get [~(type) /key] | eq [::path/KeyNotInMapError :b])
    | proves :KeyNotInMapError/namesThePathItFollowed
        |~~ The refusal holds the path the read followed. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1}} | get /a/c !| get /path | eq /a/c)
    | proves :KeyNotInMapError/suggestsTheNearestKey
        |~~ The keys of the map nearest in spelling are suggested. ~~|
        ~({:emails [] :age 3} | get /email !| get /nearest | eq [:emails])
    | proves :KeyNotInMapError/readsTwoFieldsInOneStep
        |~~ Two fields of one record are read in one step. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | get [/a /b] | eq [1 2])
    | proves :SubjectHasNoPartsError/readsTwoFieldsInOneStep
        |~~ Two fields of one record combine in one step by `mulOf`. ~~|
        ~({:qty 2 :price 3} | mulOf /qty /price | eq 6)
    | proves :SubjectHasNoPartsError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number has no fields. ~~|
        ~(42 | get /foo
          !| get [~(type) /segment /actualType] | eq [::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError :foo ::number])
    | proves :SubjectHasNoPartsError/refusesNull
        |~~ Null has no parts for `get`. ~~|
        ~(null | get /k !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError ::null])
    | proves :IndexNotIntegerError/refusesAFieldOfAVector
        |~~ A vector is read by an index, not a field. ~~|
        ~([10 20 30] | get /name !| get [~(type) /segment] | eq [::path/IndexNotIntegerError :name])
    | proves :IndexNotIntegerError/refusesAFieldOfASet
        |~~ A set is read by an index, not a field. ~~|
        ~(#[:a :b :c] | get /name !| get [~(type) /segment] | eq [::path/IndexNotIntegerError :name])
    | proves :IndexNotIntegerError/readsAFieldOfEachElement
        |~~ A field of each element is read by `map`. ~~|
        ~([{:name "a"} {:name "b"}] | map /name | eq ["a" "b"])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:path/segments
  |~~ Answers the keys and indices of the path, an index as its number
      and a key as its keyword, `/items/0 | segments` answering `[:items 0]`;
      `toPath` builds the path back from them.
      ::link~(::vec/toPath | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The keys and indices of the path. ~~| ::vec
    | proves :answersKeysAndIndices
        |~~ A key answers its keyword and an index its number. ~~|
        ~(/items/0 | segments | eq [:items 0])
    | proves :readsBackByToPath
        |~~ The segments build the path back. ~~|
        ~(/a/0 | segments | toPath | print | eq "/a/0")
    | impl ::builtin{}

